{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00488 - MONDO_16249",
  "verbalization": "Altretamine, a chemotherapy agent, is linked to Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome through a chain of indications involving Cisplatin. The evidence suggests that Altretamine may be associated with the development or progression of ovarian cancers, specifically malignancies of the ovary such as Malignant Dysgerminomatous Germ Cell Tumor and Malignant Sertoli-leydig Cell Tumor. These cancers are often treated with Cisplatin, which is known to induce Hereditary Site-specific Ovarian Cancer Syndrome. The cumulative evidence from the three paths indicates a potential mechanistic link between Altretamine and the syndrome, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated. The observed scores suggest a plausible relationship, but further research is needed to confirm the direct causal pathway and to understand how Altretamine influences the development of this syndrome. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Cisplatin in mediating the effect of Altretamine, though the specific molecular mechanisms underlying this relationship remain unclear.",
